/*
Problem: Diameter of Binary Tree
Platform: LeetCode (LC 543)
Topic: Binary Tree / DFS / Recursion

---------------------------------------------------------

Approach:

The diameter of a binary tree is the
length of the longest path between
any two nodes.

Important:

The path does NOT necessarily pass
through the root.

Diameter is measured in:

Number of EDGES.

---------------------------------------------------------

Key Observation:

For every node:

The longest path passing through it is:

height(left subtree)
+
height(right subtree)

because:

Longest path

left deepest node
        ↑
      current
        ↓
right deepest node

---------------------------------------------------------

Idea:

While computing the height of each node,
also compute the diameter passing through
that node.

Maintain a global variable:

ans

that stores the maximum diameter found.

---------------------------------------------------------

Height Function:

height(root)

returns

Maximum height from root to any leaf.

Formula:

height(root)

=

max(leftHeight,
    rightHeight)

+ 1

---------------------------------------------------------

While returning height:

Update:

ans = max(ans,
          leftHeight + rightHeight)

Since:

leftHeight + rightHeight

represents the diameter passing
through the current node.

---------------------------------------------------------

Example:

        1
       / \
      2   3
     / \
    4   5

--------------------------------

Node 4

left = 0

right = 0

height = 1

--------------------------------

Node 5

height = 1

--------------------------------

Node 2

left = 1

right = 1

diameter through 2 = 2

height = 2

--------------------------------

Node 3

height = 1

--------------------------------

Node 1

left = 2

right = 1

diameter through 1 = 3

Maximum = 3

Answer:

3 edges

---------------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Every node is considered as the
highest point of a possible longest path.

The global maximum among all such
paths is the diameter.

---------------------------------------------------------

Time Complexity:

O(n)

Each node is visited exactly once.

---------------------------------------------------------

Space Complexity:

O(h)

Recursive stack.

Worst case:

O(n)

Balanced tree:

O(log n)

---------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:

    int ans = 0;

    // Returns height of the subtree
    int height(TreeNode* root) {

        if (root == NULL)
            return 0;

        int leftHt = height(root->left);
        int rightHt = height(root->right);

        // Update maximum diameter
        ans = max(ans, leftHt + rightHt);

        // Return height of current node
        return max(leftHt, rightHt) + 1;
    }

    int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode* root) {

        height(root);

        return ans;
    }
};
